    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the OIML certificate.\n\nThe fragment is part 2 of 4 of certificate R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1. This is a water meter certificate (R49).\n\nContent present in this fragment:\n1. Certificate number: R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\n2. Software specification (section 2.2) — software versions table\n3. Accountable alarms (section 2.3)\n4. Technical and metrological data (section 3) — three tables for three variants:\n   - 3.1: UWM DN15, Q₃=1.6 m³ (actually Q3=1.6 m³/h), software and checksum 13 25667\n   - 3.2: UWM DN15, Q₃=2.5 m³, software and checksum 13 25667\n   - 3.3: UWM DN20, Q₃=2.5 m³, software and checksum 13 25667\n\nThe software table has:\n- V1.3, DN 15: checksums 21574 and 25667\n- V1.3, DN 20: checksums 22681 and 25667\n\nRemarks: See tables in point 3.\n\nInteresting — the display shows \"13\" and checksum; type plate shows \"SW 13\". So the software version V1.3 is displayed as \"13\".\n\nNow the three metrological tables:\n\n**Table 3.1: UWM DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h:**\n- Nominal diameter DN: 15 mm\n- Material of body: brass / plastic\n- Permanent flowrate Q3: 1.6 m³/h\n- Minimum flowrate Q1: 12.8, 10, 8, 6.4, 5.08, 4, 3.2 l/h (7 values)\n- Transitional flowrate Q2: 20.48, 16, 12.8, 10.24, 8.13, 6.4, 5.12 l/h (7 values)\n- Overload flowrate Q4: 2 m³/h\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 125, 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500 (7 values)\n- Ratio Q2/Q1: 1.6\n- Connection thread: G ¾ B; G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\n- Construction length L: From 110 to 190 mm\n- Pressure loss class Δp: 0.40 bar; Δp40\n- Battery: 2 or 3 pcs; Umin 2.7V, Umax 3.7V, life time up to 16 years\n\nSo this is a family of models: for DN15 Q3=1.6, there are 7 ratio variants (R 125–500). Each ratio corresponds to a different Q1/Q2 pair. The model axis here is the R ratio (Q3/Q1): R125, R160, R200, R250, R315, R400, R500.\n\n**Table 3.2: UWM DN15, Q3=2.5 m³/h:**\n- DN: 15 mm\n- Q3: 2.5 m³/h\n- Q1: 15.625, 12.5, 10, 7.94, 6.25, 5, 3.97, 3.125 l/h (8 values)\n- Q2: 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10, 8, 6.35, 5 l/h (8 values)\n- Q4: 3.125 m³/h\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500, 630, 800 (8 values)\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6\n- Connection thread: G ¾ B; G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\n- Construction length: 110–190 mm\n- Pressure loss: 0.40 bar Δp40\n- Battery: same\n\n**Table 3.3: UWM DN20, Q3=2.5 m³/h:**\n- DN: 20 mm\n- Q3: 2.5 m³/h\n- Q1: 20, 15.625, 12.5, 10, 7.94, 6.25, 5 l/h (7 values)\n- Q2: 32, 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10, 8 l/h (7 values)\n- Q4: 3.125 m³/h\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 125, 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500 (7 values)\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6\n- Connection thread: G 1 B\n- Construction length: 110–190 mm\n- Pressure loss: 0.40 bar Δp40\n- Battery: same\n\nSo the model family has three variants (by the table titles): \n1. UWM DN15 Q3=1.6 m³/h (checksum 25667 family; actually software \"13 25667\")\n2. UWM DN15 Q3=2.5 m³/h\n3. UWM DN20 Q3=2.5 m³/h\n\nBut within each variant, there are also R-ratio sub-variants. How to model this?\n\nApproach: model_family.models with model_id like \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6 R125\", etc.? Or three models with config_level for ratio axis?\n\nGiven the schema, I think the cleanest approach:\n- model_family.family_name: \"UWM\" (the type designation is UWM)\n- models: three entries? Or 22 entries (7+8+7)?\n\nActually, let me think about what varies. The three table titles define three hardware/config variants: (DN15, Q3=1.6), (DN15, Q3=2.5), (DN20, Q3=2.5). Within each, R ratio variants exist, each with distinct Q1 and Q2 values. The R ratio variant likely corresponds to different accuracy class... no wait, for water meters R is the flow ratio which defines the measuring range; all are typically class 2 for R49.\n\nI think a reasonable extraction:\n- model_family.models: list of all variants keyed by (DN, Q3, R). E.g. \"DN15-Q3_1.6-R125\". That's 22 models. That's comprehensive but verbose.\n\nAlternatively: models = 3 (the table-title variants), and config_level entries for ratio axis within each model. But config_level has a single \"axis\" and values with \"condition\". We could have:\n- config_level: attribute \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", axis \"ratio_q3_over_q1 (within model)\", values: [{condition: \"DN15 Q3=1.6 | R=125\", value: 12.8}, ...]\n\nHmm. Actually maybe the cleanest: treat each (DN, Q3) combination as a model, and use matrix_tables to preserve the three tables verbatim, plus model_level for the shared type-level attributes per model (DN, Q3, Q4), and config_level for the R-ratio-dependent attributes (Q1, Q2).\n\nLet me use matrix_tables for the three tables (they're multi-dim: attribute rows × R columns), and populate model_level with per-model attributes:\n- nominal_diameter_dn: 15/15/20 mm\n- permanent_flowrate_q3: 1.6/2.5/2.5 m³/h\n- overload_flowrate_q4: 2/3.125/3.125 m³/h\n- material_of_body: brass / plastic (all)\n- ratio_q2_over_q1: 1.6 (all)\n- connection_thread: G ¾ B / G ¾ B / G 1 B — note the DN15 tables say \"G ¾ B<br>G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\" (two options?), DN20 says \"G 1 B\". Hmm, connection thread is in mm per unit column but G threads... whatever, value as string.\n- construction_length_l: {min:110, max:190} mm (all)\n- pressure_loss: 0.40 bar, class Δp40\n- battery: 2 or 3 pcs, Umin 2.7V, Umax 3.7V, life up to 16 years\n\nAnd config_level for Q1/Q2 vs R ratio:\n- attribute: minimum_flowrate_q1, axis: \"ratio_q3_over_q1\", values with condition like \"DN15 Q3=1.6 | R125\" value 12.8.\n\nActually wait — maybe simpler and more aligned: model_level entries for q1 and q2 with model ids that encode the full variant. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe schema says model_level[] is \"attribute × model matrix\" with values[]: {model, value}. If I define models as the 22 full variants, then model_level q1 has 22 entries; q2 22 entries; q3 22; dn 22... That's verbose but complete and unambiguous. But matrix_tables already preserve the tables.\n\nHmm, the instruction in rules: \"Lists of values. 25,30,50,60,75,90 (multiple Emax values) is a MODEL MATRIX — emit model_level entries, NOT a list-as-value.\" So multiple Q1 values in a row = model matrix.\n\nGiven both structures are encouraged (matrix_tables AND model_level in the R137 example), I'll do:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"UWM\"\n- models: 3 models — \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6\", \"UWM DN15 Q3=2.5\", \"UWM DN20 Q3=2.5\"\n\nHmm but then R variants are hidden. Alternatively 22 models. Let me think about which is more useful/correct.\n\nThe R ratio (Q3/Q1) is a genuine configuration axis: a buyer picks DN, Q3, and R. The type plate would presumably show all. The certificate approves all these combinations.\n\nI think the best schema-fitting approach:\n- models: 3 (per metrological table), model_id e.g. \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6 m³/h\"\n- model_level attributes: dn, q3, q4, connection_thread, etc.\n- config_level: for q1 and q2, axis = \"ratio_q3_over_q1\", condition = e.g. \"model=DN15 Q3=1.6; R=125\". This fits rule 5: \"attributes vary by configuration (not by model...)\" — well, R could be considered part of the model spec. But the schema supports config axis with condition strings.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider: perhaps treat R as part of model identity: model_id \"DN15-Q3_1.6-R125\". Then model_level:\n- q1: 22 values\n- q2: 22 values\n- q3: 22 values (1.6 ×7, 2.5 ×15)\n- dn: 22 values\n- q4: 22 values\n- connection_thread: 22 values (mostly same)\n...\n\nThat's a lot of redundancy. The matrix_tables preserve the tables fully. I'll go with the hybrid: 3 models, model_level for per-model attributes, config_level for R-dependent q1/q2, matrix_tables for full preservation.\n\nHmm, but actually the R axis differs per model (R125–500 for table 1, R160–800 for table 2, R125–500 for table 3). Config_level condition can encode model too: condition \"DN15 Q3=1.6 | R125\". Let me just use condition strings like \"DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h, R=125\".\n\nLet me now define canonical labels:\n- nominal_diameter_dn (value 15/20, unit mm)\n- material_of_body (\"brass / plastic\")\n- permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h)\n- minimum_flowrate_q1 (l/h — could use \"L/h\"; I'll use unit_symbol \"l/h\" as in doc)\n- transitional_flowrate_q2 (l/h)\n- overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h)\n- ratio_q3_over_q1 (unit: R, dimensionless ratio — unit_symbol \"R\"? The table says unit \"R\". I'll use unit_symbol null or \"R\". The doc lists unit \"R\". I'll use \"R\".)\n\nHmm, rule says unit_symbol like \"°C\",\"V\",\"kg\" or null. \"R\" is fine as a raw symbol.\n\n- ratio_q2_over_q1 (value 1.6, dimensionless)\n- connection_thread (string \"G ¾ B\" etc.) — the unit column says mm but G threads are inch-based; keep value as string, unit null. Actually the table says unit mm. But \"G ¾ B\" isn't a mm value. I'll keep unit_symbol null and value \"G ¾ B\" / \"G 1 B\". For DN15 variants there are two lines: \"G ¾ B\" and \"G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\". Could be thread options. I'll represent as string \"G ¾ B; G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\" — hmm. Or list? Value must be number/range/string. I'll use a string. Maybe \"G ¾ B or G 7/8 B – G ¾ B\". Keep close to source: \"G ¾ B / G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\". I'll do value: \"G ¾ B; G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\"? Hmm, that's awkward. Let me just use \"G ¾ B / G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\".\n\nActually, wait: the OCR might be showing two rows or an alternative. The original: \"G ¾ B<br>G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\". Possibly connection thread options: G¾B, or G7/8B–G¾B (a union nut arrangement). I'll keep as string.\n\n- construction_length_l: {min: 110, max: 190} mm\n- pressure_loss: 0.40 bar; pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"\n- battery_quantity: \"2 or 3 pcs\"? Battery: value... battery_cell_count? I'll do battery: {value: \"2 or 3 pcs\"} — hmm, structured values. Maybe two entries:\n  - battery_quantity: {value: {min: 2, max: 3}} — it's \"2 or 3 pcs\", an either/or, not a range. Rule says \"2 or 3\" isn't a range. Keep as string? I could do battery: {value: \"2 or 3 pcs; Umin 2.7 V, Umax 3.7 V, lifetime up to 16 years\"} — too mushed.\n  \n  Better split:\n  - battery_quantity: value 2 or 3 → hmm. I'll use value: \"2 or 3\", unit_symbol: \"pcs\".\n  - battery_voltage_min: 2.7 V → battery_supply_voltage: {value: {min: 2.7, max: 3.7}, unit_symbol: \"V\"} labeled battery_voltage\n  - battery_lifetime: {value: {min: null, max: 16}, unit_symbol: \"years\"} with note \"up to\" → max 16, min null.\n\nThese are type_level (same across all models): material, ratio_q2_over_q1, construction_length, pressure_loss, battery.\n\nType_level vs model_level: attributes constant across models go to type_level. Material, q2/q1 ratio, connection thread varies (DN15 vs DN20!), construction length same, pressure loss same, battery same.\n\nSo model_level: nominal_diameter_dn, permanent_flowrate_q3, overload_flowrate_q4, connection_thread, and... minimum/maximum flowrates per config.\n\nWait, but Q4: for Q3=1.6 → Q4 = 2 m³/h (Q4/Q3 = 1.25); for Q3=2.5 → Q4 = 3.125. So Q4 is model-dependent → model_level.\n\nNow model ids: \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6\" etc. Let me name them: \"DN15 Q3=1.6 m³/h\", \"DN15 Q3=2.5 m³/h\", \"DN20 Q3=2.5 m³/h\". Prefix with type UWM? The type is UWM for all. I'll use \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6\" etc. Hmm, keep simpler: \"DN15_Q3_1.6\"? The R60 example used \"Emax=25t\" format. I'll use \"DN15 Q3=1.6 m3/h\"? Avoid superscript issues — \"m³/h\" is fine Unicode. I'll use model_id \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6 m³/h\". Hmm, maybe \"DN15-Q3=1.6\". Let me pick: \"DN15 Q3=1.6\", \"DN15 Q3=2.5\", \"DN20 Q3=2.5\". Clean.\n\nConfig_level entries:\n\nattribute: minimum_flowrate_q1, unit l/h, axis: \"ratio_q3_over_q1\", values: for each model×R combination:\n\nDN15 Q3=1.6: R125→12.8, R160→10, R200→8, R250→6.4, R315→5.08, R400→4, R500→3.2\nDN15 Q3=2.5: R160→15.625, R200→12.5, R250→10, R315→7.94, R400→6.25, R500→5, R630→3.97, R800→3.125\nDN20 Q3=2.5: R125→20, R160→15.625, R200→12.5, R250→10, R315→7.94, R400→6.25, R500→5\n\ncondition strings: \"DN15 Q3=1.6, R=125\" etc.\n\nSimilarly q2:\nDN15 Q3=1.6: 20.48, 16, 12.8, 10.24, 8.13, 6.4, 5.12\nDN15 Q3=2.5: 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10, 8, 6.35, 5\nDN20 Q3=2.5: 32, 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10, 8\n\nDecimal conversions: 12,8→12.8; 20,48→20.48; 10,24→10.24; 8,13→8.13; 6,4→6.4; 5,12→5.12; 15,625→15.625; 12,5→12.5; 7,94→7.94; 3,97→3.97; 3,125→3.125; 12,7→12.7; 6,35→6.35.\n\nMatrix tables: three tables, one per section. columns: characteristic, unit, then R values. The tables are transposed: rows are characteristics, columns are R ratios. For matrix_tables I'll preserve:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"Technical and metrological data UWM DN15 Q3=1.6 m³/h\",\n  \"columns\": [\"characteristic\", \"unit\", \"R125\", \"R160\", \"R200\", \"R250\", \"R315\", \"R400\", \"R500\"],\n  ...\n}\n\nHmm, rows as objects. Let me structure rows with keys: characteristic, unit, r125, r160, ..., r500. Values: q1 row: 12.8, 10, 8, 6.4, 5.08, 4, 3.2. Rows that apply to all (dn, material, q3, q4, etc.) have single spanned value — I'll put the value under... hmm, spanned across columns. I could put value in a \"value\" field and null for per-R columns. Maybe simpler: rows objects with keys: characteristic, unit, r125...r500, and for spanned rows put the value in each R column? Or add key \"common_value\"? \n\nLet me design: rows: [{\"characteristic\": \"nominal_diameter_dn\", \"unit\": \"mm\", \"value\": 15}, ...] for common rows, and for per-R rows include the r-columns. Mixed shapes are okay-ish but messy. Alternatively give every row all R keys plus \"common\" key:\n\n{\"characteristic\": \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"unit\": \"m³/h\", \"common\": 1.6, \"r125\": null, ...}\n\nHmm. Or simply put the common value repeated in each column — that misrepresents but preserves data. I think best: use keys \"characteristic\", \"unit\", \"common\", \"r125\", ..., \"r800\" (where applicable). For per-R rows, common=null and the r keys filled. That's clean enough.\n\nActually maybe simpler to name columns as in the schema example: columns: [\"characteristic\", \"unit\", \"r125\", \"r160\", ...]. And rows with those keys, null where not applicable/spanned... but spanned means \"applies to all\", so null is wrong semantically. I'll add \"common\" for spanned values and set r-keys null. I'll describe in \"description\" field: \"Rows with 'common' apply to all ratio variants; r* columns give values per ratio Q3/Q1.\"\n\nFootnotes: none present in this fragment. Red stamp is an artifact — ignore. Page footers — ignore.\n\nSoftware spec: This is section 2.2. How to fit? The schema doesn't have a software section explicitly. Could put into characteristics type_level: software_version: \"V1.3\" and checksums. The checksums vary: DN15 → 21574 & 25667; DN20 → 22681 & 25667. So checksum is model/config dependent:\n- checksum_dn15: 21574 and 25667 (two checksums — probably firmware and... two software components). \n- checksum_dn20: 22681 and 25667.\n\nActually looking: DN15 has rows 21574 and 25667; DN20 has 22681 and 25667. So 25667 common, other differs. Interesting — displayed form: \"13 and checksum (21574/25667/22681)\". The table headers: Software versions | Size | Checksum | Remarks. Size = DN 15 / DN 20 (\"size\" here = nominal diameter!). So V1.3 software, size DN15, checksums 21574 and 25667; size DN20, checksums 22681 and 25667.\n\nI could emit this as a matrix_table \"Software versions\" and/or model_level entries. Since the two \"sizes\" correspond to DN15/DN20 which map to my models (DN15 Q3=1.6, DN15 Q3=2.5 both DN15; DN20 Q3=2.5). The checksum depends only on DN. \n\nI'll add a matrix_table:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Software versions\",\n  \"columns\": [\"software_version\", \"size\", \"checksum\", \"remarks\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"size\": \"DN 15\", \"checksum\": 21574, \"remarks\": \"See tables in point 3\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"size\": \"DN 15\", \"checksum\": 25667, \"remarks\": \"See tables in point 3\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"size\": \"DN 20\", \"checksum\": 22681, \"remarks\": \"See tables in point 3\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"size\": \"DN 20\", \"checksum\": 25667, \"remarks\": \"See tables in point 3\"}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Software version V1.3 shown as '13' on display and 'SW 13' on type plate; checksums displayed/printed as 21574/25667/22681.\"\n}\n\nAnd type_level characteristics:\n- software_version: {value: \"V1.3\", footnote_markers: []} — displayed as \"13\". Maybe also software_version_display: \"13\" / \"SW 13\". I'll add software_version: \"V1.3\" and a note? There's no notes field for type_level; only value/unit/footnote_markers. I could add characteristic \"software_version_displayed\" value \"13\" and \"software_version_on_type_plate\" \"SW 13\". That's reasonable.\n\nChecksums: model_level? The checksum depends on DN, not on full model. Could do config_level with axis \"nominal_diameter\":\n- attribute: software_checksum, axis: \"size\", values: [{condition: \"DN 15\", value: 21574}, {condition: \"DN 15\", value: 25667}, {condition: \"DN 20\", value: 22681}, {condition: \"DN 20\", value: 25667}]. Two values per condition — allowed? values[] is a list, so multiple entries with same condition okay.\n\nAlternatively matrix_table suffices. I'll do both matrix_table and model_level entries for checksums:\n- attribute software_checksum, model_level: values for \"DN15 Q3=1.6\": [21574, 25667]? Value must be single object. Could do two entries per model with same attribute. Hmm, values[] allows multiple entries per model:\n{\"model\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6\", \"value\": 21574}, {\"model\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6\", \"value\": 25667}, {\"model\": \"DN15 Q3=2.5\", \"value\": 21574}, {\"model\": \"DN15 Q3=2.5\", \"value\": 25667}, {\"model\": \"DN20 Q3=2.5\", \"value\": 22681}, {\"model\": \"DN20 Q3=2.5\", \"value\": 25667}\n\nThat's legit. I'll include it. Also software_version is same for all: type_level \"V1.3\".\n\nAccountable alarms section 2.3 — descriptive text; could go in a description? certificate-level or certified_type.description is \"free-text description if present\". This fragment doesn't give general description, but the alarms text is a functional description. I might include certified_type.description? It's about the calculator detecting faults. Hmm — it's part of the fragment content. I could include it as certified_type.description: \"During the measuring process the calculator and indication device automatically detects fault conditions and stops the measurement reporting an alarm indication on the display (accountable alarms). See user manual issued by the manufacturer.\" That seems reasonable. Or omit. I'll include it — it's certified_type info.\n\nCertificate-level fields present: number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\". Should I parse scheme A? The number contains \"-A-\" indicating scheme A. The recommendation id R49. But part 1 (not shown) likely has issuing authority etc. This fragment only repeats the certificate number in headers. Should I emit certificate.number and recommendation? The instruction: \"produce a JSON object ... for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present.\" The certificate number IS present (in page headers). I'll include certificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\" and scheme \"A\" (derivable from number). Recommendation R49: the number implies R49 but the recommendation section isn't explicitly present. Hmm. The certificate number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\" strongly implies R 49. I think including recommendation {id: \"R49\"} is defensible; maybe include scheme \"A\" too? The scheme letter in the number is certificate scheme. For R49/2013 (water meters), accuracy classes not mentioned here. I'll include recommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013?} — wait, \"2013\" in \"R49/2013\" is likely the edition year of the recommendation or the certificate series. OIML R49 edition is 2013? Hmm, R49 \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water\" — editions 2013? Actually OIML R49-1:2013? Hmm, R49 was published 2013? Let me think... OIML R 49-1 edition 2013? I believe there's R49-1:2013 (E). Yes, R 49-1:2013 exists. But I shouldn't over-infer. The number format R49/2013-A-... — for other certs like \"R60/2000-GB1-...\" the 2000 is the recommendation edition year. So edition: 2013? Hmm wait, but R49/2013 could be edition. I'll include edition: 2013? Risky but the pattern is standard. Actually, hold on — is it worth including? The fragment doesn't explicitly state the recommendation. Given \"Don't fabricate\", deriving R49 from the cert number is a mild inference. I'll include recommendation {id: \"R49\"} and edition 2013? I'll include id only... Hmm, the edition in \"R49/2013\" — in NMi certificates like \"R49/2013-...\" the /2013 refers to the edition of the recommendation. I'll include edition: 2013. Actually to be safe I'll include {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\"}? The scheme \"A\" from cert number — certificate scheme A means single issuer. The instruction schema: recommendation.scheme. Hmm, recommendation scheme? R60 certs have scheme field? Under recommendation: scheme. I'll set scheme \"A\" consistent with cert number. Actually maybe leave recommendation minimal: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013}. And certificate.scheme: \"A\". \n\ncertificate fields: number, scheme \"A\", page_total? Page footer says \"Page 4 of 10 pages\" and \"Page 5 of 10 pages\" → page_total: 10. Include that. member_state: stamp says SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY → member_state Slovakia? That's from the stamp artifact; it's real content indicating issuing authority Slovakia. But issuing_authority section (name/address) not fully present. Hmm. The stamp \"SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY – X – SLM\" suggests issuing authority is Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM). I could set certificate.member_state: \"Slovakia\" and issuing_authority.name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology\"? It's a red stamp artifact but conveys the authority. Given \"ignore image markers\" refers to ![](page=...) lines, the stamp text is content. I'll include issuing_authority.name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology\" — hmm, but rule 8: don't fabricate. The stamp is present; the name is readable. I'll include name \"Slovak Legal Metrology\" (SK). Maybe safer to only include member_state \"Slovakia\" at certificate level? I'll include both, minimal: issuing_authority.name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology\". Eh — I think it's fine and helpful.\n\nActually, let me be careful: the fragment is \"part 2 of 4\", page 4-5 of 10. Other parts have the full header. For this fragment, including certificate.number and page_total from footers is right. I'll include issuing_authority with name from stamp, oiml_issuer_id? Not present (no ID like \"SK1\" — actually the cert number contains \"SK1\" which might be issuer id... \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\" — SK1 could be the Slovak issuer identifier. Hmm, oiml_issuer_id format is like \"NMi\", \"PTB\", \"SLM\"... \"SK1\"? Not certain. Omit.)\n\ncertified_type: category \"Water meter\" (R49). type_designations: [\"UWM\"]. module_designation: null — omit or \"Not applicable\"? The water meter is a complete measuring instrument; no module info. I'll omit or set null. I'll omit.\n\nHmm, but wait — UWM is the water meter type designation. Model family name \"UWM\".\n\nNow connection thread label: \"connection_thread\". Value for DN15 models: \"G ¾ B; G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\". Hmm the \"¾\" char. Keep as \"G ¾ B / G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\"? I'll use value: \"G ¾ B or G 7/8 B – G ¾ B\"? Let me keep close to source: \"G ¾ B / G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\". Actually the <br> suggests two lines = two options. I'll write \"G ¾ B; G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\".\n\nHmm, alternatively connection thread might be listed as two possible connections. Keep string.\n\nPressure loss row: \"Pressure loss class Δp\" with unit \"bar / -\" and value \"0,40 / Δp40\". Split:\n- pressure_loss: 0.40 bar (type_level)\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\" (type_level)\n\nBattery row:\n- battery_quantity: \"2 or 3\" pcs — hmm value \"2 or 3\", unit \"pcs\". Or battery: string. I'll do:\n  - battery_quantity: {value: \"2 or 3\", unit_symbol: \"pcs\"} — hmm, is \"2 or 3\" acceptable as string value? Yes, strings allowed. \n  - battery_voltage: {value: {min: 2.7, max: 3.7}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - battery_lifetime: {value: {min: null, max: 16}, unit_symbol: \"years\"} — \"life time up to 16 years\" → max 16. unit \"years\" or \"a\"? Use \"years\".\n\nConstruction length: {min: 110, max: 190} mm. \"From 110 to 190\" → range. type_level.\n\nNow assemble model_level:\n\nmodels: \n1. model_id \"DN15 Q3=1.6\" — label? Maybe \"UWM DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h\". attributes: {}? The schema: models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. I can put per-model attributes right in the model entries OR in characteristics.model_level. To avoid duplication, I'll put attributes in model entries? The schema example for R60 only had model_id. And characteristics.model_level carries the matrix. I'll keep models light: model_id + label, and put attributes in model_level.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- nominal_diameter_dn: [{DN15 Q3=1.6: 15}, {DN15 Q3=2.5: 15}, {DN20 Q3=2.5: 20}], unit mm\n- permanent_flowrate_q3: [1.6, 2.5, 2.5], unit m³/h\n- overload_flowrate_q4: [2, 3.125, 3.125], unit m³/h\n- connection_thread: [string values], unit null\n- software_checksum: six entries as above\n- software_version? same for all → type_level.\n\ntype_level:\n- software_version: \"V1.3\" — displayed as 13. I'll add software_version_displayed: \"13\", software_version_type_plate: \"SW 13\"? Maybe just software_version \"V1.3\". I'll include software_version and checksums via model_level. Maybe include \"software_display_format\": \"13 and checksum\"? Too noisy. Keep software_version: {value: \"V1.3\"}.\n- material_of_body: \"brass / plastic\"\n- ratio_q2_over_q1: 1.6 (dimensionless; source unit \"-\")  unit_symbol null\n- construction_length: {110,190} mm — label construction_length_l? The L is the symbol. Use \"construction_length_l\". Hmm canonical labels: \"construction_length\" fine. I'll use construction_length.\n- pressure_loss: 0.4 bar\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"\n- battery_quantity: \"2 or 3\" pcs\n- battery_voltage: {2.7, 3.7} V\n- battery_lifetime: {null, 16} years\n- ratio_q2_over_q1 1.6\n\nconfig_level:\n- minimum_flowrate_q1, axis \"ratio_q3_over_q1\", values with condition \"DN15 Q3=1.6, R=125\" → 12.8 l/h etc.\n- transitional_flowrate_q2, same axis.\n\nAlso maybe add ratio_q3_over_q1 as model_level? No — it's the config axis; the conditions carry it.\n\nLet me double check values against OCR:\n\nTable 3.1 (DN15, Q3=1.6):\nQ1: 12,8 / 10 / 8 / 6,4 / 5,08 / 4 / 3,2 → 12.8, 10, 8, 6.4, 5.08, 4, 3.2 ✓\nQ2: 20,48 / 16 / 12,8 / 10,24 / 8,13 / 6,4 / 5,12 → 20.48, 16, 12.8, 10.24, 8.13, 6.4, 5.12 ✓\nR: 125,160,200,250,315,400,500 ✓\nQ4 = 2 m³/h ✓ (2/1.6 = 1.25 ✓)\n\nTable 3.2 (DN15, Q3=2.5):\nQ1: 15,625 / 12,5 / 10 / 7,94 / 6,25 / 5 / 3,97 / 3,125 → 15.625, 12.5, 10, 7.94, 6.25, 5, 3.97, 3.125 ✓\nQ2: 25 / 20 / 16 / 12,7 / 10 / 8 / 6,35 / 5 → 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10, 8, 6.35, 5 ✓\nR: 160,200,250,315,400,500,630,800 ✓\nQ4 = 3,125 → 3.125 ✓ (2.5×1.25=3.125 ✓)\n\nCheck R for Q3=2.5, Q1=15.625: 2.5 m³/h = 2500 l/h; 2500/15.625 = 160 ✓. R=800: 2500/3.125=800 ✓. Good.\n\nTable 3.3 (DN20, Q3=2.5):\nQ1: 20 / 15,625 / 12,5 / 10 / 7,94 / 6,25 / 5 → 20, 15.625, 12.5, 10, 7.94, 6.25, 5 ✓\nQ2: 32 / 25 / 20 / 16 / 12,7 / 10 / 8 ✓\nR: 125,160,200,250,315,400,500 ✓ (2500/20=125 ✓)\n\nConsistency Q2/Q1 = 1.6: 12.8×1.6=20.48 ✓; 15.625×1.6=25 ✓; 20×1.6=32 ✓. \n\nNow matrix_tables: I'll create three, one per section 3.1/3.2/3.3. Columns: [\"characteristic\", \"unit\", \"common\", \"r125\", \"r160\", \"r200\", \"r250\", \"r315\", \"r400\", \"r500\"] (add r630, r800 for table 2). Rows:\n\nTable 3.1:\n- nominal_diameter_dn, mm, common 15\n- material_of_body, null, \"brass / plastic\"\n- permanent_flowrate_q3, m³/h, 1.6\n- minimum_flowrate_q1, l/h, common null, r125 12.8, r160 10, r200 8, r250 6.4, r315 5.08, r400 4, r500 3.2\n- transitional_flowrate_q2, l/h, 20.48, 16, 12.8, 10.24, 8.13, 6.4, 5.12\n- overload_flowrate_q4, m³/h, 2\n- ratio_q3_over_q1, R, null common, r125 125, ..., r500 500\n- ratio_q2_over_q1, null, 1.6\n- connection_thread, null, \"G ¾ B; G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\"\n- construction_length, mm, {min 110 max 190}? In matrix table rows keep as string \"From 110 to 190\"? I'd use {\"min\":110,\"max\":190}. JSON object fine.\n- pressure_loss_class_dp, \"bar / -\", \"0.40 / Δp40\"? Better split rows: pressure_loss, bar, 0.4; pressure_loss_class, null, \"Δp40\".\n- battery, null, \"2 or 3 pcs; Umin 2.7 V, Umax 3.7 V, lifetime up to 16 years\"? For matrix preservation, keep raw-ish string: \"2 or 3 pcs; Umin 2,7 V, Umax 3,7 V, life time up to 16 years\". Hmm, decimal commas — rule says convert. But it's a string anyway. I'll write \"2 or 3 pcs; Umin 2.7 V, Umax 3.7 V, life time up to 16 years\".\n\nThis makes matrix tables somewhat redundant with characteristics but rule says preserve. Good.\n\nInclude column_units? Schema example had column_units. Not in the yaml schema listing but in example. I'll include column_units where sensible: [null, null, null, \"l/h\"?...] Hmm, units vary per row, so column_units: null. I'll skip column_units, or set null. Skip it.\n\nFor the software table, matrix_tables entry as designed.\n\nNow certificate object:\n{\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"page_total\": 10,\n  \"member_state\": \"Slovakia\"\n}\n\nWait — should I include \"Rev. 1\" in number? The full string is \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\". The revision might belong in revision_history (Rev. 1 = revision \"1\", changes unknown). Since part 1 probably has issue date etc., here I only have the number with Rev. 1. I'll keep number as \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\" and add revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"1\"}]? Changes and date unknown → omit changes/date? Schema: revision, date, changes. Can include just revision. Hmm. Alternatively number includes \"Rev. 1\". I think cleaner: number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\" as printed? The instruction example formats revision as string \"0\". I'll do number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\" and revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"1\"}]. Both defensible. I'll go with that.\n\nrecommendation: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013}? Hmm — \"R49/2013\" the 2013 might be part of cert numbering (year of certificate registration?) Common OIML CS cert numbers: \"R60/2000-XX-...\" where 2000 = edition of R60 in force. For R49, edition 2013 exists (R49-1/2/3/4:2013? Actually R49-1:2013? I recall OIML R 49-1 edition 2013? Hmm, there's R49-1 2013? Let me recall: OIML R 49-1:2013 \"Water meters for cold potable water — Part 1: Metrological and technical requirements\"? I believe yes, 2013 edition exists. Hmm, but also R49-1 2003? There's R49 2000 edition? The 2003? I think R49-1:2013 (E) is real.) I'll include edition: 2013. Scheme \"A\" at certificate level.\n\nHmm, actually the instruction schema for recommendation includes \"scheme\" too. certificate.scheme = \"A\" (from number). I'll put scheme only in certificate.\n\nissuing_authority: name \"Slovak Legal Metrology\" — from stamp. I'll include name only. Actually the stamp says \"SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY – X – SLM\". The official body might be \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\" / \"Slovak Metrology Institute\"? Wait — the actual Slovak issuer for OIML CS is \"Slovak Legal Metrology\" (SLM), a.s.? Hmm, actually Slovakia's issuer was \"Slovak Legal Metrology\" — yes, SLM, n.o. (Slovenská legálna metrológia). I'll write name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\". Hmm, adding \"(SLM)\" is from stamp. Fine.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"UWM\"]\n- description: alarms text? I'll include the accountable alarms description. Actually maybe put it as description: \"Accountable alarms: during the measuring process the calculator and indication device automatically detects fault conditions and eventually stops the measurement reporting an alarm indication on the display (see user manual issued by the manufacturer).\"\n\nmodel_family: family_name \"UWM\", models 3.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\ntest_reports: none