bject as instructed.\n\nSimilarly model_level values: schema shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} without unit in example, but the entry has unit_symbol at attribute level (\"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"). For model_level, the schema yaml: values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]} — no unit per value; unit goes at entry level (\"unit_symbol\"? the yaml shows attribute, unit, values — example JSON uses \"unit_symbol\" at entry level). OK so model_level: unit at entry level. config_level: rule 5 says per-value. Slight inconsistency but I'll follow rule 5 literally: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\n\nHmm wait, re-reading rule 5: \"use characteristics.config_level with axis describing the dimension. Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\" Yes, per value. OK.\n\nFor model_level checksums — value plain numbers, entry-level unit null.\n\nSoftware checksum model_level:\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"software_checksum\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": null,\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6\", \"value\": 21574},\n    {\"model\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6\", \"value\": 25667},\n    {\"model\": \"DN15 Q3=2.5\", \"value\": 21574},\n    {\"model\": \"DN15 Q3=2.5\", \"value\": 25667},\n    {\"model\": \"DN20 Q3=2.5\", \"value\": 22681},\n    {\"model\": \"DN20 Q3=2.5\", \"value\": 25667}\n  ]\n}\n\nWait — the software table's \"Size\" column is DN 15 / DN 20, and it's section 2.2 before the model tables. The mapping checksum→model: DN15 covers both DN15 models; DN20 the third. Good.\n\nHmm, is \"size\" here maybe the software size? No — DN is the meter size. OK.\n\nType-level: software_version \"V1.3\". Also maybe \"software_version_display\": \"13\". I'll include both software_version and a characteristic \"software_version_displayed_as\" value \"13\"? Keep it: I'll include \"software_version\": {\"value\": \"V1.3\"} and skip display variants to reduce noise. Hmm, the display form is genuinely useful for verification (\"13 and checksum\"). I'll add \"software_version_displayed\": {\"value\": \"13\"} and mention in matrix table description. Fine, include both.\n\nNow model entries with labels:\n{\"model_id\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6\", \"label\": \"UWM DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h (software 13, checksum 21574/25667)\"}? Too wordy; label simpler: \"UWM DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h\". And attributes {} — or put dn/q3/q4 in attributes per model instead of model_level? I'll keep attributes minimal {} and use model_level. Actually maybe put the key per-model attributes into attributes{} AND skip model_level for those? Duplication risk. Schema says models[].attributes{} exists for \"one entry per variant: model_id, label, attributes{}\". It's an alternative to model_level. To be safe and consistent with examples (which use model_level), I'll use model_level and leave attributes out or {}.\n\nI'll include attributes with the per-model values anyway? No — choose model_level only, attributes omitted.\n\nHmm, actually let me include both? No. Just model_level.\n\nCertificate description of sections 3.x titles: \"software and checksum 13 25667\" for all three tables. Fine.\n\nLet me now also double-check: table 3.1 title says \"Q₃=1,6 m³\" (missing /h) — it's Q3=1.6 m³/h. Fine.\n\nPressure loss row unit column: \"bar<br>-\". Value \"0,40<br>Δp40\". Split into pressure_loss 0.4 bar and pressure_loss_class Δp40.\n\nBattery: \"2 or 3 pcs<br>Umin 2,7V, Umax 3,7V, life time up to 16 years\".\n\nNow the matrix_tables rows format. Let me write them.\n\nMatrix table 1:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Technical and metrological data — UWM DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h, software and checksum 13 25667\",\n  \"columns\": [\"characteristic\", \"unit\", \"r125\", \"r160\", \"r200\", \"r250\", \"r315\", \"r400\", \"r500\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"characteristic\": \"water_meter_type\", \"unit\": null, \"common\": \"UWM\", \"r125\": null, ...}\n  ]\n}\n\nHmm including \"common\" key not in columns list — add \"common\" to columns. Columns: [\"characteristic\", \"unit\", \"common\", \"r125\", \"r160\", \"r200\", \"r250\", \"r315\", \"r400\", \"r500\"]. Rows include only relevant keys? Rows as objects can omit keys or set null. I'll include all keys with null for clarity. Actually that's a lot of typing but fine.\n\nLet me simplify: rows objects include \"characteristic\", \"unit\", and either \"common\" or r-keys, omitting inapplicable ones. JSON objects with heterogeneous keys per row — acceptable. I'll omit nulls to keep size manageable.\n\nTable 1 rows:\n1. {characteristic: \"nominal_diameter_dn\", unit: \"mm\", common: 15}\n2. {characteristic: \"material_of_body\", unit: null, common: \"brass / plastic\"}\n3. {characteristic: \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", unit: \"m³/h\", common: 1.6}\n4. {characteristic: \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", unit: \"l/h\", r125: 12.8, r160: 10, r200: 8, r250: 6.4, r315: 5.08, r400: 4, r500: 3.2}\n5. {characteristic: \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", unit: \"l/h\", r125: 20.48, r160: 16, r200: 12.8, r250: 10.24, r315: 8.13, r400: 6.4, r500: 5.12}\n6. {characteristic: \"overload_flowrate_q4\", unit: \"m³/h\", common: 2}\n7. {characteristic: \"ratio_q3_over_q1\", unit: \"R\", r125: 125, r160: 160, r200: 200, r250: 250, r315: 315, r400: 400, r500: 500}\n8. {characteristic: \"ratio_q2_over_q1\", unit: null, common: 1.6}\n9. {characteristic: \"connection_thread\", unit: null, common: \"G ¾ B; G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\"}\n10. {characteristic: \"construction_length_l\", unit: \"mm\", common: {\"min\": 110, \"max\": 190}}\n11. {characteristic: \"pressure_loss\", unit: \"bar\", common: 0.4}\n12. {characteristic: \"pressure_loss_class_dp\", unit: null, common: \"Δp40\"}\n13. {characteristic: \"battery\", unit: null, common: \"2 or 3 pcs; Umin 2.7 V, Umax 3.7 V, life time up to 16 years\"}\n\ndescription: \"Values under 'common' apply to all ratio variants; columns r125–r500 correspond to ratio Q3/Q1 = 125–500.\"\n\nTable 2 (DN15 Q3=2.5): r160..r800 columns.\nQ1: r160 15.625, r200 12.5, r250 10, r315 7.94, r400 6.25, r500 5, r630 3.97, r800 3.125\nQ2: r160 25, r200 20, r250 16, r315 12.7, r400 10, r500 8, r630 6.35, r800 5\ncommon: dn 15, material, q3 2.5, q4 3.125, q2/q1 1.6, thread, length, pressure, battery.\n\nTable 3 (DN20 Q3=2.5): r125..r500.\nQ1: r125 20, r160 15.625, r200 12.5, r250 10, r315 7.94, r400 6.25, r500 5\nQ2: r125 32, r160 25, r200 20, r250 16, r315 12.7, r400 10, r500 8\ncommon: dn 20, material, q3 2.5, q4 3.125, q2/q1 1.6, thread \"G 1 B\", length, pressure, battery.\n\nSoftware matrix table:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Software versions\",\n  \"columns\": [\"software_version\", \"size\", \"checksum\", \"remarks\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"size\": \"DN 15\", \"checksum\": 21574, \"remarks\": \"See tables in point 3\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"size\": \"DN 15\", \"checksum\": 25667, \"remarks\": \"See tables in point 3\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"size\": \"DN 20\", \"checksum\": 22681, \"remarks\": \"See tables in point 3\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"size\": \"DN 20\", \"checksum\": 25667, \"remarks\": \"See tables in point 3\"}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Software version V1.3 (displayed as '13' on the screen and 'SW 13' on the type plate) with checksums 21574/25667 (DN 15) and 22681/25667 (DN 20).\"\n}\n\nType-level characteristics final list:\n- software_version: {value: \"V1.3\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- software_version_displayed: {value: \"13\", ...}\n- material_of_body: {value: \"brass / plastic\"}\n- ratio_q2_over_q1: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: null}\n- construction_length: {value: {min:110, max:190}, unit_symbol: \"mm\"}\n- pressure_loss: {value: 0.4, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n- pressure_loss_class_dp: {value: \"Δp40\", unit_symbol: null}\n- battery_quantity: {value: \"2 or 3\", unit_symbol: \"pcs\"}\n- battery_voltage: {value: {min: 2.7, max: 3.7}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_lifetime: {value: {min: null, max: 16}, unit_symbol: \"years\"}\n\nShould software_version be type_level given checksums are model_level? Yes.\n\nModel-level:\n- nominal_diameter_dn, mm: DN15 Q3=1.6 → 15; DN15 Q3=2.5 → 15; DN20 Q3=2.5 → 20\n- permanent_flowrate_q3, m³/h: 1.6, 2.5, 2.5\n- overload_flowrate_q4, m³/h: 2, 3.125, 3.125\n- connection_thread, null: \"G ¾ B; G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\", same, \"G 1 B\"\n- software_checksum: 6 entries\n\nunit_id: null everywhere (leave unless certain). I'll include unit_id: null in each structured value? Rule 2 shape includes unit_id. The example in rule 7 footnote shows only value/unit_symbol/footnote_markers. Including \"unit_id\": null everywhere adds bulk. Rule 2: \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\" including unit_id. I'll include unit_id: null in type_level values. For model_level values entries ({model, value}) the schema doesn't include unit at value level — unit at entry level. So model_level: {\"attribute\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": null, \"values\": [...]}. Good — put unit_id at entry level.\n\nFor config_level values: {condition, value, unit_symbol} — include unit_id null too? Keep {condition, value, unit_symbol} per rule 5. I'll add unit_id: null? That's 44 value objects... bloat. Rule 5 only lists condition, value, unit_symbol. I'll follow exactly: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\n\nFor type_level: include value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers omitted when empty? Shape: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers[]}. I'll include footnote_markers only when non-empty (footnotes none here → omit). Include unit_id: null.\n\nModels array:\n[\n  {\"model_id\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6\", \"label\": \"UWM DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h\"},\n  {\"model_id\": \"DN15 Q3=2.5\", \"label\": \"UWM DN15, Q3=2.5 m³/h\"},\n  {\"model_id\": \"DN20 Q3=2.5\", \"label\": \"UWM DN20, Q3=2.5 m³/h\"}\n]\n\ncertified_type.description: include accountable alarms text: \"Accountable alarms: during the measuring process the calculator and indication device detects automatically if a fault condition occurs and eventually stops the measurement reporting an alarm indication on the display. See user manual issued by the manufacturer.\"\n\nAssemble final JSON. Ensure valid JSON: no trailing commas, proper unicode (¾, ³, Δ fine in JSON).\n\nconnection_thread string: source \"G ¾ B<br>G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\". I'll use \"G ¾ B / G 7/8 B – G ¾ B\"? Keep hyphen as in source: \"G ¾ B; G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\". Use that.\n\nCertificate:\n{\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"page_total\": 10,\n  \"member_state\": \"Slovakia\",\n  \"date_issued\": null → omit\n}\n\nrevision_history: [{\"revision\": \"1\"}] — changes/date unknown; include just revision. OK.\n\nrecommendation: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013}. Hmm, edition as int. Fine. Actually should I be cautious about edition? \"R49/2013\" strongly suggests edition 2013 of R49. I'll include.\n\nissuing_authority: {\"name\": \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\"}. From stamp. I'll include.\n\nOrder the JSON keys sensibly: certificate, issuing_authority, certified_type, model_family, characteristics, matrix_tables, recommendation, revision_history.\n\nDouble-check numbers as JSON numbers: 12.8, 10, 8, 6.4, 5.08, 4, 3.2 etc. All good.\n\nOne more consideration: \"l/h\" vs \"L/h\" — keep \"l/h\" as source.\n\nBattery lifetime: \"life time up to 16 years\" → {min: null, max: 16}. Good.\n\nWrite final JSON.",
